{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1692361792/3/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"9f669b64\" lines=\"26-48\">\n<description>\nPush the central solid rectangle to the grid edge, splitting the perpendicular target rectangle around its path.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- There are exactly three monochrome foreground objects arranged on one\n  horizontal or vertical axis.\n- The middle object on that axis is a solid rectangular moving object.\n- One end object is a solid rectangle or bar whose long side is perpendicular\n  to the movement axis; this is the target to split.\n\nRule\n- Move the middle rectangle along the axis toward the target until it is\n  flush with the grid edge in that direction.\n- Clear the original middle rectangle and the original target.\n- Split the target through the moving rectangle's two-cell-wide path and\n  shift the two halves one cell outward perpendicular to the movement.\n- Keep the other end object unchanged.\n\nKey insight\n- The non-target end object acts as a pusher; the central rectangle travels\n  away from it through the perpendicular target, opening a matching gap.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"5e6bbc0b\" lines=\"42-56\">\n<description>\nCompact the checkerboard's moving color toward the edge marked by the teal pixel, leaving a maroon swept trail on the marker line.\n\nInput structure\n- A blue/black checkerboard with exactly one teal marker replacing a blue pixel on a non-corner edge.\n- The marker's checkerboard parity identifies the moving color (blue); the opposite parity is background (black).\n\nRule\n- If the marker is on the left or right edge, compact moving-color pixels in every row toward that side.\n- If the marker is on the top or bottom edge, compact moving-color pixels in every column toward that side.\n- Preserve the teal marker at its edge cell.\n- On the marker's row or column, color cells swept by shifted moving pixels maroon unless the final compacted line occupies them.\n\nKey insight\nThe marker selects both the compression direction and the checkerboard color class that moves; maroon records the movement trail only along that marked line.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"b10624e5\" lines=\"24-39\">\n<description>\nReplicate a motif across quadrants with scaling and reflection keyed to red anchor blocks.\n\nInput structure:\n- Grid divided into four quadrants by a full-row and full-column divider of a single non-bg color.\n- Each quadrant contains a red (2) square anchor block of varying size.\n- The top-left quadrant also has a colored motif attached to its red anchor.\n\nRule:\n- Use the top-left quadrant's motif as the source pattern.\n- For each other quadrant, scale the motif by the ratio of that quadrant's red block size to the source red block size.\n- Reflect horizontally for right-side quadrants, vertically for bottom quadrants (both for bottom-right).\n- Align the scaled/reflected motif so its red region overlaps the target anchor, then stamp non-bg pixels.\n\nKey insight: Red block size encodes the scale factor; quadrant position encodes the reflection axes.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"f28a3cbb\" lines=\"42-50\">\n<description>\nCompress each colored ray pixel inward until it touches that color's 3x3 anchor block.\nInput structure\n- A uniform background grid with non-background colors.\n- Each foreground color contains one solid 3x3 anchor block; all other pixels of that color lie on straight 8-neighbor rays aimed at that block.\nRule\n- Keep anchor blocks fixed, erase loose foreground pixels, and for each ray pack its pixels into consecutive cells adjacent to the anchor along the same ray.\nKey insight\n- The scattered pixels are displaced pixels on visible row, column, or diagonal sightlines back to their same-colored 3x3 block.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"5ecac7f7\" lines=\"23-36\">\n<description>\nCombine the left, middle, and right ordered objects from three separated panels.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid is three HxH panels separated by one-cell vertical separator bars.\n- The background is the dominant color, and each panel contains exactly three same-color 4-connected foreground objects.\n\nRule\n- Build a new HxH background canvas.\n- From the left panel copy its leftmost object, from the middle panel copy its middle object, and from the right panel copy its rightmost object.\n\nKey insight\n- The separator bars create three aligned layers, and the panel's own left-to-right object order selects which object survives in the merged output.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"8e301a54\" lines=\"8-23\">\n<description>\nMove every foreground object straight downward by a number of rows equal to its own pixel count.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has one dominant background color.\n- The foreground is made of separated 4-connected monochromatic objects.\n- Objects may move partly below the bottom edge of the grid.\n\nRule:\n- Clear the canvas to the background.\n- For each foreground object, compute its area in pixels.\n- Redraw the object at the same columns and that many rows lower, clipping pixels below the grid.\n\nKey insight:\n- The downward displacement is the object's own area; color and shape only identify which pixels move together.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"5d588b4d\" lines=\"10-28\">\n<description>\nExpand the top-left horizontal bar into a wrapped sequence of increasing and decreasing foreground runs.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has a background color and exactly one foreground color.\n- All foreground pixels form one contiguous horizontal bar in the top row.\n- The bar starts at the left edge; its length is the control value n.\n\nRule:\n- Build a one-dimensional sequence of foreground runs with lengths\n  1, 2, ..., n, n-1, ..., 2, 1.\n- Separate adjacent runs by one background pixel.\n- Wrap the sequence row-major across the input width and pad the final row\n  with background pixels.\n\nKey insight:\nThe output preserves the input width; the bar length controls the run-length\ndiamond, and the apparent 2D pattern is just that 1D sequence wrapped into rows.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"1e5d6875\" lines=\"26-45\">\n<description>\nPaint a recolored diagonal copy from each L triomino source object.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- The only foreground objects are 4-connected red and grey L triominoes,\n  each occupying three pixels of a 2x2 box.\n\nRule:\n- Grey L objects paint a yellow copy translated one diagonal step toward\n  their missing 2x2 corner.\n- Red L objects paint a green copy translated one diagonal step away from\n  their missing 2x2 corner.\n- Copies are clipped by the grid boundary; red-derived green copies are\n  painted after grey-derived yellow copies where the projections collide.\n\nKey insight:\n- The missing corner of each 2x2 L object identifies its diagonal axis, and\n  the source color determines which side of that axis receives the copy.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"f341894c\" lines=\"63-78\">\n<description>\nReorient each two-pixel blue/pink segment so it reads pink then blue when viewed from its border marker.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is dominant, with one border-only marker color and blue/pink foreground segments.\n- Every marker lies on the outer border and points inward along its row or column.\n- Along exactly one inward ray from each marker is a single adjacent two-pixel blue/pink segment.\n\nRule\n- For each marker, look inward perpendicular to the border side containing the marker.\n- Rewrite the two blue/pink pixels on that ray so the nearer one is pink and the farther one is blue.\n- Keep marker pixels, background pixels, and all other pixels unchanged.\n\nKey insight\n- The marker gives a local viewing direction; the segment's required order is relative to that marker, not to global row or column order.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"981add89\" lines=\"10-29\">\n<description>\nExtend each top-row control pixel downward as a vertical toggle ray in its own color.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Non-background pixels on the top row are control pixels, each identifying\n  one column and one ray color.\n- Foreground regions below may intersect these control columns.\n\nRule:\n- For every top-row control pixel, process the cells below it in the same\n  column down to the bottom edge.\n- A cell that already has the control color is erased to the background.\n- Every other cell on that downward ray is painted with the control color.\n- The top-row control pixels themselves remain unchanged.\n\nKey insight:\nThe top row is a set of vertical ray launchers; each ray toggles only cells\nmatching its own color and overwrites all other colors along that column.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"2faf500b\" lines=\"30-44\">\n<description>\nRemove each fracture marker line and push the surrounding object pixels one cell away from it.\n\nInput structure\n- A background grid with 8-connected foreground objects made from a body color and a rarer marker color.\n- Each object has a non-square bbox; the marker pixels form one complete fracture line across the shorter bbox axis.\n\nRule\n- Erase all marker pixels.\n- For a wide object, each row has one marker; body pixels left of it shift one cell left and body pixels right of it shift one cell right.\n- For a tall object, each column has one marker; body pixels above it shift one cell up and body pixels below it shift one cell down.\n\nKey insight\n- The marker line is a crack through the object, so the two sides separate by one pixel perpendicular to the crack.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"d749d46f\" lines=\"32-51\">\n<description>\nPack each solid foreground rectangle twice: wide across the top and tall from the bottom.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid has one background color and one foreground color; the foreground forms\nseparate solid rectangles, ordered from left to right.\n\nRule\nBuild a 10-row background canvas whose width is the packed width of the wide\nversions of the rectangles. For each source rectangle, paint a top-aligned\nrectangle of shape min(h,w) by max(h,w), with one background column between\nconsecutive rectangles. Then, starting again from the left edge, paint a\nbottom-aligned rectangle of shape max(h,w) by min(h,w) for each source rectangle,\nagain separated by one background column.\n\nKey insight\nThe input rectangles are not copied in their original orientations; each object\ncontributes one horizontal-facing and one vertical-facing normalized rectangle,\npreserving left-to-right object order.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"13\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"996ec1f3\" lines=\"16-31\">\n<description>\nCompress the noisy four-region board split by a solid cross into a 3x3 color map.\n\nInput structure:\n- A single full row and a single full column have the same solid separator color.\n- The separator cross divides the input into four nonempty rectangular regions.\n- Each region has a unique dominant color, with other colors acting as noise.\n\nRule:\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
